WEBVTT

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.120
Winter.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:51.840
Its icy embrace can transform our countryside... ..almost beyond recognition.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:01:05.360
And as we humans seek sanctuary indoors and by cosy fires, you'd be forgiven for thinking the animal kingdom simply lies low, tries to survive and waits for the warmth of spring to arrive.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:12.760
But even in winter, there are wildlife spectacles to be discovered all over our island.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:17.520
Some you need to know where to look, but others can be right on your own doorstep.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:30.520
I'm at the Cotswold Water Park, transformed after decades of industrial-scale quarrying into a series of lakes and ponds that have returned the landscape to its former glory.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:36.040
As a result, it's now teeming with wildlife, especially birds.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:44.560
I grew up just down the road from here, but funnily enough this water park is a bit of a mystery to me.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:54.920
But I've been promised the chance to spot some of the migratory birds that stop off here, and to find out about the life both above and below the water.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:05.080
And while I'm here, I'll be looking back at some of the Countryfile team's favourite encounters with some wonderful winter wildlife.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:12.000
Matt checks in on a bit of Irish wildlife.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.440
We've seen a few aeroplanes land and they don't even twitch an ear.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.480
No, they don't.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.320
They seem to like the noise and the vibrations, almost like a bit of a thrill for them.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:27.360
Wildlife cameraman Richard Taylor-Jones is in Scotland with some unusually easy-to-find subjects.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:35.160
How's about that for a truly Scottish winter scene?

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.720
A beautiful herd of red deer stags.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.320
There've got to be at least 20 up there.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:47.800
And Adam is on Exmoor, revisiting the ponies that inspired his interest in rare breeds.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.440
These animals are quite wild.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:54.400
They live out on the moor all year round, and they are perfectly designed for it.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.240
They've been living out here for hundreds, if not thousands of years.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:18.240
The Cotswold Water Park - a series of large lakes created by decades of gravel extraction.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:25.320
There are now more than 150 lakes across 40 square miles, spanning the three counties Gloucestershire, Oxfordshire and, where I am today, Wiltshire.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:39.600
The patchwork of woodland, reedbeds and water make it ideal for all kinds of wildlife.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:42.120
But I'm here to see the big winter visitors - the birds.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:50.120
More than 200 species call the park home during the year.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:54.280
At the moment, that's around 20,000 wintering water birds.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:08.800
With numbers like that, it should be fairly easy to do some spotting, but I've enlisted the help of Kim Milsom from the Cotswold Water Park Trust to help me with the task of identification.

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:19.079
And we need to tread carefully and quietly, because 20,000 birds could become zero if we spook them and they bid a hasty getaway.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.160
Kim, what have we got out here?

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.720
There's coot, to begin, isn't there?

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:29.360
Yeah, we have got some coot, and then we've got a nice selection of duck today.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:29.960
Right.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:33.160
Most of the ones we can see at the moment are wigeon.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:38.040
The wigeon have come in for the winter, they are one of the early ones to arrive.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:38.640
Yeah.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:39.680
What else have we got?

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:43.400
There's some mallard, then there's some goldeneye.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:46.200
They are one of the diving ducks.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:49.960
They will dive down to catch their food and they'll go under for quite some time.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:54.920
There's the two main types of duck, you've got dabbling ducks that feed near the surface, and diving ducks.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:58.560
And the goldeneye are one of the divers, they'll go down quite deep.

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:00.120
They like deep water.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:07.920
It seems kind of surprising that a warm-blooded animal would come down here for warmer conditions.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:12.040
It seems amazing they can get enough calories to survive these kinds of temperatures, because it is cold today, isn't it?

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:18.720
Well, it is very cold today, but where they've come from, everything is probably frozen solid, and the colder the weather gets, the more duck we get here.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:21.360
They just go wherever they can find food and shelter.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:27.880
If it gets incredibly cold here and we freeze up, then they'll just move further south.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:30.560
But most of them will stay for the winter.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:31.520
Some of these are well-travelled birds.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:37.880
Goldeneye come to our island from Scandinavia and northern Russia.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:40.600
Wigeon come from similar colder climes like Iceland.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:47.240
And shovellers, here most of the year, have a huge shovel-like bill to scoop up their food from the surface.

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:56.560
Later I'll be discovering more about how the water park makes this the perfect home for birds and other wildlife.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:11.240
But first, to County Antrim in Northern Ireland, where Matt went in search of an ancient wild species with fluctuating fortunes.

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:13.680
The Irish hare.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:24.880
Only found on the Emerald Isle, the Irish hare was once widespread, but it suffered a long-term decline over the last 100 years.

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:33.800
Dr Neil Reid from Queen's University and is an expert in one of Ireland's best-kept secrets.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:40.360
Genetically, how different are they to the other hares around the UK?

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:55.280
They're a type of mountain hare, so they are a subspecies of mountain hare, similar to the Scottish mountain hare, but they are much larger, they are behaviourally very distinct, so they occur right down on the seashore to the tops of mountains, and they are truly native to Ireland.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:59.800
They are really very fast, they can move at about 45mph.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:00.520
Can they?

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:07.200
And they are incredibly agile, so they can turn 180 degrees on the spot at full speed, and that allows them to escape from foxes and other predators.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:07.920
Wow!

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:21.520
Neil is currently looking at the causes of the rise and fall of hare numbers across 12 sites in Northern Ireland.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:28.240
The population can double within two years, and then subsequently halve again within the next two years.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:32.040
This is where these canes come into it.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:33.640
Neil marks out a grid system around the camera.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:39.520
By measuring the distance of the animals from the camera we can calculate the density of the animals within the field.

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:44.760
He then repeats this grid system 12 times with 12 cameras over the whole of the field.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:51.240
Using a bit of clever trigonometry, he can then estimate the number of hares in the whole area.

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:56.880
They are triggered by movement, so if you simply move through the field, that will start taking a video.

00:07:55.960 --> 00:08:05.920
The footage is then used to assess the impact that things like predators and changes in agriculture have on the population.

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:11.720
Neil's also found Irish hares have set up home somewhere rather more surprising.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:15.960
Belfast International HARE-port!

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:30.200
Despite its busy runways and round-the-clock operations, the airport has an average density of hares around ten times higher than in the wider countryside.

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:38.039
I'm off on a runway safari with John Jeffers from the Operations team.

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:42.640
Have they got free run of the airport?

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:44.960
They certainly have, yes.

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:52.200
It's so wonderful just to see them bobbing about all over the place, and really, they are here because you're managing for a completely different reason.

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:54.960
That's correct, yes.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:59.400
We operate what the civil aviation authority would call a long grass policy.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:08:59.520
Right.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:03.960
And that's to deter birds, believe it or not.

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:07.800
So, for 11 months of the year, we try and keep the grass at a length of 150 millimetres.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:08.640
Is that so that birds don't flock?

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:12.320
That's exactly right, birds don't like long grass.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:17.040
That habitat just seems to suit the hare.

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:18.880
It gives them enough cover for themselves and the leverets.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:22.800
And so do you do anything to increase or decrease the numbers?

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:23.400
Absolutely nothing.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:24.040
Nothing at all?

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:26.400
Absolutely nothing, no.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:27.600
We neither encourage them or discourage them in any shape or form.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:31.280
We've seen a few aeroplanes land.

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:33.240
They don't even twitch an ear.

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:33.880
No, they don't.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:36.160
They seem to like the noise and the vibrations, almost like a bit of a thrill for them.

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:40.040
MATT CHUCKLES There's one there having a bit of an altercation with a crow.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:40.240
Look at them two, having a go!

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:49.600
CROW CAWS You must get quite a nice reaction from passengers.

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:57.920
Yeah, the passengers talk about them all the time, and they're even in the car parks, so when you're parking your car you see them, and then when you're taxiing out to go on your holidays, you see them along the taxiways.

00:09:57.080 --> 00:09:58.280
So they're almost an icon of the airport.

00:09:57.440 --> 00:09:59.000
Yeah.

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:06.720
It seems these Irish hares have no plans to jet off from the airport any time soon.

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:20.600
Today I'm in a corner of Wiltshire that is part of the vast Cotswold Water Park.

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:26.040
1,000 hectares of water - that's around 2,500 acres.

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:32.720
It's perfect for wintering birds like teal, wigeon, and gadwall.

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:38.200
These lakes are created when the quarrying companies move out.

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:45.720
After the valuable gravel and sand has been extracted, the pits fill with water and nature slowly takes over.

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:48.600
Sometimes with a helping hand.

00:10:54.880 --> 00:11:02.240
This quarry closed in 2003 and will become part of the wider water park over the next few years.

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:10.760
Ben Welbourn is from the charity the Cotswold Water Park Trust.

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:16.920
It's their job to advise and supervise when the quarries are returned to nature.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:24.840
The quarry companies will have quite a strict restoration plan they've got to stick to, and that ensures they create the best habitat, the most diverse habitat.

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:26.560
What do they have to do once they finish with their business?

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:34.800
Hopefully, a rich landscape that has got varying depths of water, a mosaic of habitats, and attracting all sorts of different species.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:38.480
A lot of the water park is fun, isn't it - the kayaking, the canoeing.

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:42.960
What percentage is there of fun stuff to serious stuff?

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:53.360
There's plenty of fun stuff if that's what you want to come here to do, but it's about balancing that also with the importance of biodiversity and public access and allowing the public to enjoy the landscape that is developing.

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:53.680
Yeah, absolutely.

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:00.680
The Trust's wardens are out in force today.

00:12:00.280 --> 00:12:08.800
One of their regular tasks is keeping this patchwork of habitat in peak condition for all the wildlife here.

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:11.480
Industrious around here.

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:13.520
Some willow for us to chop.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:14.240
Yep.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:14.880
Why do you chop this all down?

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:23.880
Well, we try not to chop it all down, but it is mainly to allow the reedbeds to establish.

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:24.480
Oh, OK.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:27.800
Reed bed is one of our biodiversity action plan target habitats.

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:30.480
It's really important for all sorts of species.

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:34.600
So we come in and take out patches of the willow wherever we can.

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:37.640
What happens if you didn't take this out?

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:38.040
It would take over quite quickly.

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:39.240
Right.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:41.600
And then what do you do with this willow?

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:46.040
We have a nice big bonfire to keep the volunteers warm!

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:47.120
I tell you, today you absolutely need it.

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:47.760
You really do.

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:51.040
In the middle of winter like this, what sort of wildlife might you guys get to see while you're working away?

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:57.840
Well, if we're really lucky, we might see one of our other target species, and that is the bittern.

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:58.040
Oh, wow!

00:12:57.160 --> 00:13:00.400
I didn't know you had bittern here.

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:00.800
We do, yeah.

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:01.640
I'll come down more often.

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:03.640
They haven't bred yet, but we're hoping it won't be far off.

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:05.520
How fantastic!

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:06.680
What else?

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:12.400
We're starting to see more and more otters in the water park, and of course they love reedbeds, too.

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:12.800
Great.

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:17.280
So, it's clearly working, all this clearance you're doing.

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:18.720
It's creating these great habitats for all sorts of different species.

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:22.000
It is working, we just can't take our eye off the ball.

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:23.080
Absolutely.

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:28.440
Work like this should help bring in even more wildlife to the water park.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:36.680
But there are some animals that are quite happy with just a blustery beach, as Julia discovered when she headed to Norfolk to watch a wildlife spectacular.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:43.120
This is a seasonal wonder that isn't difficult to find here, although I'm told it should be approached with caution.

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:54.240
This exposed peninsula of salt marsh, shingle and golden sand is Blakeney Point.

00:13:53.280 --> 00:14:01.320
And at this time of year, it's one great big maternity ward for a colony of grey seals.

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:08.880
All these new mums and pups need someone to keep an eye on them, and that is Edward Stubbings' job.

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:19.280
These seals are three miles away at the end of this beach, so not many people come across them.

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:23.720
Just as well, as these mums do not react well to people or dogs.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:31.920
Originally came with more of a bird-based background, and have been working with seals more and more as the seal population has increased.

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:33.040
Learning on the job.

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:34.800
Learning on the job, and learning very quickly.

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:46.160
Twice a week, Eddie comes to these windswept sands to count how many more pups have been born.

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:49.280
With such a rapidly expanding colony, it's vital that Eddie keeps an eye on things.

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:49.680
Here we go.

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:52.400
What a spectacle!

00:14:54.920 --> 00:15:00.080
Yeah, some have got mum with them, some haven't.

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:00.600
Where do you start when it comes to counting?

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:05.960
We're going to be on the top of the dunes, we're not going to approach the seals.

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:07.280
Unless we get blown into them!

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:08.120
Exactly, yes.

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:11.720
We'll walk alongside each other along the top of the dunes, both with a clicker.

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:13.360
You'll be counting on the right, I'll be counting on the left.

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:15.080
Are you sure that's all right for you?!

00:15:14.120 --> 00:15:15.920
You've got more than I have.

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:18.280
You've got about three!

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:21.120
I thought it would be a good baptism by fire.

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:28.880
And you don't click the clicker until you're level with the pup, and then you walk on, and you don't click the next pup until you're level with it.

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:35.320
With the cold swell of the North Sea starting to push up the beach, we get clicking.

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:39.560
So, from here we've got one, two, three, four...

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:43.040
I'm counting that one next to mum, there.

00:15:48.200 --> 00:15:49.960
And then I can see another two, three...

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:51.080
Three more there, yeah.

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:52.840
..Four, five, six.

00:15:53.600 --> 00:16:04.040
As I continue to click away, it's not hard to believe that around 40% of the world's population of grey seals breed in the UK.

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:07.320
So, one, two, three.

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:08.080
Three.

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:08.960
OK, there we go.

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:09.200
Yeah.

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:13.280
How old do you think that one is there?

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:15.640
That one's probably just three days old, tops.

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:21.640
And why are they all here, Eddie?

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:31.920
Taking it back to the 20th century, there were certain laws that were passed to protect seals, and from that point onwards, they did start increasing.

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:34.920
And then they started working their way down the East Coast.

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:39.720
And then around the millennium, they hit Blakeney, and since then have just flourished.

00:16:38.840 --> 00:16:46.560
Presumably if the colony keeps on expanding, there's a danger that seals and humans will clash.

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:47.760
Yeah, absolutely.

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:51.000
We are already seeing the evidence of it, and the seals are spreading out across the whole reserve.

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:53.040
It's becoming harder to manage.

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:58.320
The team don't advise people to walk here during breeding season, as the seals are easily disturbed.

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:04.680
There are organised trips for enthusiasts.

00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:05.079
Time to tot up our numbers and see how many new arrivals there are.

00:17:07.319 --> 00:17:12.760
593 pups on this beach!

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:19.680
Well, there's either something in the Norfolk air or something in the Norfolk water, but it is working, whatever it is.

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:19.920
Phenomenal.

00:17:23.839 --> 00:17:28.640
The number of pubs being born is up.

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:34.040
To keep track of the colony, Eddie plans to photo ID some of the mums.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:38.240
Is it easy to identify them?

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:38.840
It's not easy.

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:40.480
Especially not here when there are so many cows on one beach.

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:42.160
But there are a few things you can look for.

00:17:41.480 --> 00:17:47.480
The side of the neck, they seem to be quite distinctive markings on the neck.

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:53.360
If you have any with marks or scars, then take a photo of that area.

00:17:53.600 --> 00:18:00.800
Eddie hopes to take on some more experienced volunteers for this project, but for now he'll have to make do with me.

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:13.600
I'm meant to be taking pictures of adult females, but the pups are so cute, I can't help myself.

00:18:15.440 --> 00:18:16.520
And there are lots to take photos of.

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:25.720
Then Eddie spots one he recognises.

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:32.440
So that's the hope of the photo ID programme, to be able to trace females like this one, year-on-year.

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:33.480
Yeah.

00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:39.040
It will teach us more about the colony, build up a picture of the cows that are pupping here.

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:45.160
Julia in Norfolk, watching and counting some of our most striking winter wildlife.

00:18:52.520 --> 00:19:06.240
Back in the Wiltshire corner of the Cotswold Water Park, I've been spotting some of the many wintering birds that are making a stopover here, from far-flung and less forgiving climes, like Russia and the Arctic.

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:11.160
But I'm going to find out what lies beneath the water, or rather this chap is.

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:13.520
This is Jack Perks.

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:23.720
He joined us on Countryfile in the warm, sunny days of July when he helped Joe film the trout and grayling of Derbyshire's chalk streams.

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:29.200
Jack describes himself as a fish twitcher, and he uses some rather special underwater cameras to study the behaviour of freshwater fish.

00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:29.960
Jack, how's it going?

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:30.960
Yeah, good, thanks.

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:36.680
No fish for us today, then.

00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:39.880
No, after something feathery, for a change.

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:42.200
Right, similar techniques, though?

00:19:42.200 --> 00:19:45.360
Yeah, using a decoy.

00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:48.400
When I do the fish, I don't tend to take plastic fish with me.

00:19:47.560 --> 00:19:58.200
So the idea is using this fake rubber duck, placing it out with a camera attached to it and the other ducks will come and investigate and say hello, hopefully.

00:19:57.360 --> 00:19:59.760
You sound confident.

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:01.760
That's cam one.

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:02.360
Cam one.

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:02.920
Yeah.

00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:05.080
This is cam two, obviously the hi-tech version.

00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:08.520
And then the really, really technical bit, and I need to patent this, I think.

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:09.080
Camera three.

00:20:08.120 --> 00:20:09.800
Is camera three, which...

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:10.680
I love it, Jack!

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:15.400
Just goes like that.

00:20:14.520 --> 00:20:19.280
I think it will take off, personally.

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:21.400
I don't know what you think.

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:21.960
You've made my day!

00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:23.360
We'll see it on the catwalk soon, that one.

00:20:22.480 --> 00:20:25.200
The question is, is it going to work?

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:33.840
Well, it's an experiment, really, because I've never done this before, so it is all new, so haven't got a clue if it's going to work, but I'm reasonably confident something will happen.

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:39.680
Yeah, and you've had success in the past with similar set-ups, have you?

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:50.000
Yeah, so I've done more tamer waterfowl in parks and things with birdseed to encourage them, and I've got some nice underwater footage of things like pochard diving, black swans and mute swans feeding.

00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:55.160
So I have got underwater footage of waterfowl in the past, but not the kind of wild, migratory birds.

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:55.920
So that's going to be the test today.

00:20:56.920 --> 00:21:00.080
All I'm going to say is, don't mess it up.

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:08.360
With such state-of-the-art equipment, what could possibly go wrong?

00:21:07.400 --> 00:21:09.800
Well, for a start, it looks like we scared everything away.

00:21:10.120 --> 00:21:12.720
All the birds seem to have disappeared since he's gone over there.

00:21:15.080 --> 00:21:18.920
But perhaps when they settle down, see those decoys, they'll come back.

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:19.040
You never know.

00:21:26.800 --> 00:21:31.200
That's got to convince any bird.

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:32.840
It's convincing me.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:36.920
Fingers crossed we'll have more luck later.

00:21:35.960 --> 00:21:37.560
As Jack knows, wildlife is difficult to capture at the best of times.

00:21:38.120 --> 00:21:44.040
But winter can also present unique opportunities, especially for our wildlife cameraman, Richard Taylor-Jones.

00:21:46.840 --> 00:21:57.000
When I'm in Scotland, three species really come to mind, and that's the red squirrel, the red deer and the crested tit.

00:21:56.280 --> 00:22:02.480
And winter is the best time to film them, I think, because of one simple thing - they're hungry.

00:22:10.360 --> 00:22:16.040
A very friendly group of conservationists have come to the woodland here and they've set up a feeding station.

00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:29.760
You've got this lovely big mesh of peanuts here which the birds are going to absolutely love, and then just over behind me, we have a squirrel feeding box, and the squirrel will come and sit on this platform here, use its head to flip the lid up and get to the lovely peanuts inside.

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:39.480
Well, it hasn't taken long before we've got coal tits... ..which are distinctive by the lovely white stripe down the back of their head.

00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:47.440
And then - oh, look at this!

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:51.960
Masses of long-tailed tits.

00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:54.400
They've almost...

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:54.880
Yeah, they've completely taken over the feeder.

00:22:57.200 --> 00:23:06.600
And actually, a crested tit, the bird I was after, has just snuck in whilst I wasn't looking, and it's over by the squirrel feeder.

00:23:05.680 --> 00:23:14.680
It's gone underneath the squirrel feeder and is feeding that way, because the long-tails have just completely hogged the bird feeder.

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:22.400
The crested tits are a real treat.

00:23:21.720 --> 00:23:31.400
They are specialists of the Caledonian pine forest, and there are probably only about 1,500 breeding pairs in the whole of the UK.

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:37.320
They are quite common here in Scotland, but, you know, nationally, they are incredibly scarce.

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:45.040
An absolute treat, and a real symbol of the Scottish woods here.

00:23:47.360 --> 00:23:50.080
(Here we go. )

00:23:57.840 --> 00:24:00.000
(Lovely, lovely animal. )

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:08.240
(Now, you'll notice that there's actually a bit of grey in his coat.

00:24:07.360 --> 00:24:14.640
(That's not because he is halfway between a red and a grey squirrel, (this is what happens to red squirrels in the wintertime.

00:24:13.800 --> 00:24:23.120
(They have a summer coat which they moult out in the autumn (for a thicker, warmer winter coat that has a greyish tinge to it. )

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:29.160
And he's doing exactly what I thought, he's using his head to flip up that lid and reach down to grab some nuts.

00:24:32.600 --> 00:24:42.040
You can hear the road behind me, you can hear cars whizzing behind me, so it just goes to show you, these aren't difficult animals to see.

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:47.040
You could just park up, pop out and see this very, very easily for yourself.

00:24:53.920 --> 00:24:59.560
Red squirrels and crested tits - tick!

00:24:58.600 --> 00:25:01.840
Two down, one to go - the red deer.

00:25:00.920 --> 00:25:02.600
And I have a plan.

00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:16.960
I've had a tip that just 15 minutes away on a grouse estate, I'm guaranteed great shots.

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:23.000
How's about that for a truly Scottish winter scene?

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:24.760
A beautiful heard of red deer stags.

00:25:23.800 --> 00:25:26.800
There've got to be at least 20 up there.

00:25:25.880 --> 00:25:29.760
And these are wild animals.

00:25:29.320 --> 00:25:33.880
Normally I'd never be able to get this close to them.

00:25:33.280 --> 00:25:37.720
But there's a very good reason why I can, and it's all down to the keeper here.

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:06.400
You can see that there's one of the stags here that has a really curved antler at the top.

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:09.120
He's called Droopy, apparently.

00:26:08.160 --> 00:26:15.880
And the reason he's got the antler like that is probably that it was damaged when it was growing.

00:26:14.920 --> 00:26:19.520
It's a very, very soft material as it initially comes out of the head.

00:26:18.800 --> 00:26:24.160
And it probably got a knock, and it sent it in the wrong direction.

00:26:23.360 --> 00:26:31.000
And of course, the stag will lose those antlers and then grow a whole new set next summer.

00:26:30.160 --> 00:26:34.840
So it's a deformity that probably isn't going to cause him a problem for long, if it's even caused him a problem at all.

00:26:36.560 --> 00:26:41.600
So there you go - three animals in one day.

00:26:41.920 --> 00:26:42.960
Red squirrels, red deer, and crested tits.

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:46.800
It just goes to show that winter can be a great time to get out and watch wildlife.

00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:58.000
Beautiful footage of winter wildlife captured by Richard Taylor-Jones in Scotland.

00:27:02.320 --> 00:27:12.600
Whilst our intrepid explorer Jack Perks continues his filming mission trying to capture bird behaviour under water at the Cotswold Water Park, we are off to the coast.

00:27:14.360 --> 00:27:22.000
The Wirral peninsula stretches out ten miles into the Irish Sea.

00:27:21.080 --> 00:27:24.560
This sliver of land sits quietly in the shadows of its vibrant neighbour, Liverpool.

00:27:24.200 --> 00:27:31.520
Every winter, it's home to tens of thousands of birds that flock here seeking sanctuary, as Matt discovered.

00:27:32.600 --> 00:27:43.880
This mysterious landscape is at the mercy of the tides that lap and sap its shores every single day, making the land appear and disappear, and sometimes changing its shape entirely.

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:52.920
This is the seaside town of Parkgate, a once thriving resort on the River Dee.

00:27:52.120 --> 00:27:57.600
It has all the usual holiday destination requirements - a chip shop, ice cream parlours...

00:27:57.160 --> 00:28:03.080
Only these days there seems to be one vital element missing - the sea.

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:08.720
Elizabeth, this is a seaside town, but without the sea.

00:28:09.200 --> 00:28:10.840
You're absolutely right.

00:28:10.040 --> 00:28:15.280
No, there is no sea now, it's all silted up.

00:28:14.360 --> 00:28:22.320
The sea was here, I suppose, to the last war, but since then it has just rapidly developed as marsh.

00:28:21.440 --> 00:28:22.800
And how has that happened?

00:28:22.160 --> 00:28:24.440
Several processes.

00:28:23.480 --> 00:28:33.480
All estuaries silt up because more silt is brought in by the tide than is actually carried out.

00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:35.760
But in the Dee, it has really been speeded up by man.

00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:42.320
In the early 18th century, when ships couldn't get up to Chester, they dug a new cut.

00:28:41.520 --> 00:28:46.360
Parkgate was lucky because the water actually came round and ran below us here.

00:28:45.720 --> 00:29:06.600
But then finally what did for Parkgate, I suppose, was the introduction of a very vigorous grass called Spartina which was intended to stabilise the silt, but actually spread so rapidly that it colonised all this marsh, all this marsh that you can see, all the way up, three or four miles that way.

00:29:05.880 --> 00:29:15.760
It's very odd, isn't it, when you look back and you see this lovely row of very traditional seaside buildings, and then you look out and there's just grass.

00:29:15.520 --> 00:29:19.800
It's still a place people come to, even though there's no sea.

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:20.840
There's a grey heron, there, look.

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:21.360
Oh, yes!

00:29:20.480 --> 00:29:21.680
Coming in.

00:29:20.760 --> 00:29:22.960
Beautiful.

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:33.800
The shifting silt being dragged in and out by the tide may be bad news for Parkgate holiday-makers, but this type of landscape is great for migratory birds.

00:29:34.280 --> 00:29:48.560
This is West Kirby, about eight miles from Parkgate, and here the silty flats have become the perfect winter stopover for tens of thousands of our feathered friends, transforming the Wirral into a wildlife wonderland, if you will.

00:29:52.240 --> 00:29:56.880
Matt Thomas is a coastal ranger on the Wirral.

00:29:56.000 --> 00:29:59.840
He loves this enigmatic landscape and the birds that fly here.

00:30:00.720 --> 00:30:04.120
Matt, why is this such a good stopover for all of these visiting birds?

00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:08.280
There's an amazing amount of food for them here.

00:30:07.520 --> 00:30:13.440
As you can see, there's just wide open spaces.

00:30:12.520 --> 00:30:14.440
So there's all this food, all this space.

00:30:13.600 --> 00:30:22.840
So they just congregate here in huge numbers, and they feed up - some of them as a stopover on a southward migration to Africa, others will stay here all year round.

00:30:22.120 --> 00:30:24.080
I mean, we're talking big numbers, aren't we?

00:30:23.200 --> 00:30:27.520
Yes, loads - up to 120,000 waders and wildfowl which is quite an amazing number, really.

00:30:28.760 --> 00:30:29.920
Yeah.

00:30:28.960 --> 00:30:30.160
Yeah.

00:30:29.200 --> 00:30:31.120
Well, it's coming in pretty quick, isn't it, this tide now...

00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:31.480
It is.

00:30:30.640 --> 00:30:32.000
..So we'd better clamber up these rocks.

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:32.120
Yeah.

00:30:39.520 --> 00:30:48.880
It's a real inconvenience for the birds when the tide comes in, as it covers up their watery dinner plate for hours.

00:30:48.040 --> 00:30:51.040
But they feast until the last possible moment, before the tide forces them into the air.

00:30:53.560 --> 00:30:57.560
Obviously they can't feed when the tide's in, so where do they go?

00:30:57.120 --> 00:31:00.520
Right, there's lots of high tide roosts around here.

00:30:59.720 --> 00:31:04.920
There'll be some in the marshes over there, some on the armour stone round the lake here, and lots on Hilbre Island as well.

00:31:04.280 --> 00:31:06.400
That's the island just behind us?

00:31:05.560 --> 00:31:06.840
It is, yes.

00:31:08.200 --> 00:31:17.280
Seeing the birds at close range at high tide is a possibility if you don't mind being stranded for seven hours until the tide goes out.

00:31:16.320 --> 00:31:23.440
But I'm planning to get us a private window on this secret world, by motoring across by boat - something not many people get to do.

00:31:30.560 --> 00:31:34.040
This is something new for you as well.

00:31:34.160 --> 00:31:36.280
It is, yes.

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:45.200
Normally the islands are accessed at low tide - either on foot if you're a member of the public, or as one of the coastal rangers, I tend to go out there in a Land Rover.

00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:48.720
So yeah, it's a bit of a treat for me to be out on the boat as well.

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:59.280
There's a collection of three little islands.

00:31:58.440 --> 00:32:02.800
Yes, we've got Little Eye just down there, Middle Eye in front of us, and Hilbre just there.

00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:08.040
And together they make up the Hilbre Islands Local Nature Reserve.

00:32:07.080 --> 00:32:10.920
This one here's loaded with oystercatchers, isn't it?

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:12.520
Well, you know, they're measured in the thousands at this site.

00:32:11.760 --> 00:32:14.800
So there's lots of oystercatchers, there'll be loads of curlew as well, there's some cormorant there.

00:32:14.080 --> 00:32:18.080
I mean, it's a special place, isn't it, for that.

00:32:18.640 --> 00:32:20.760
Absolutely fantastic.

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:23.960
Amazing wildlife spot.

00:32:23.320 --> 00:32:29.280
Matt on the Wirral, with a close-up look at thousands of sea birds.

00:32:28.520 --> 00:32:32.760
I'm also looking at migrating birds, but from a rather different angle.

00:32:35.080 --> 00:32:37.920
Earlier, I left Jack Perks in the water.

00:32:38.080 --> 00:32:43.200
I wasn't being cruel - although it is pretty nippy.

00:32:42.240 --> 00:32:49.480
But he was laden with cameras and gadgets to try and capture the underwater habits of the Cotswold Water Park's feathered visitors.

00:32:51.160 --> 00:32:52.720
So how did it go out there?

00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:57.120
Well, not as good as I'd hoped.

00:32:56.640 --> 00:32:58.520
'Now, come on, Jack.

00:32:57.560 --> 00:32:58.880
It can't be that bad - can it?

00:32:57.960 --> 00:32:59.320
'Well - yes, it can.

00:32:59.360 --> 00:33:03.120
'But Jack's got something else up his sleeve.'

00:33:02.600 --> 00:33:09.000
As a backup, instead, I put some cameras round the sides, just to watch if birds went in and out.

00:33:08.080 --> 00:33:10.280
Oh, OK.

00:33:09.480 --> 00:33:11.880
And we did get birds going in and out.

00:33:11.040 --> 00:33:17.960
So I do know they did land, so I know the decoys didn't scare them off or anything, they did go into the water.

00:33:17.080 --> 00:33:18.200
Cool.

00:33:17.320 --> 00:33:19.040
That's your best shot, shall we have a look?

00:33:20.520 --> 00:33:21.720
Round the back...

00:33:20.760 --> 00:33:22.200
And these guys almost hit the camera.

00:33:21.320 --> 00:33:23.000
And those were teal coming out?

00:33:22.680 --> 00:33:23.960
Yeah, they were teal.

00:33:23.680 --> 00:33:29.840
And the chances are you wouldn't have got that shot with a human by that camera.

00:33:28.920 --> 00:33:30.280
No, no, they...

00:33:29.480 --> 00:33:31.320
They wouldn't have landed at all.

00:33:30.400 --> 00:33:33.680
So it is still a different shot, but not quite what we might have wanted.

00:33:32.760 --> 00:33:36.640
I know it works in principle, so just a little bit of work on it, we'll...

00:33:35.680 --> 00:33:37.120
Just keep going with duck decoy.

00:33:36.160 --> 00:33:38.160
That's it.

00:33:41.200 --> 00:33:50.640
So Jack's happy with the principles, if not the result.

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:56.840
But at least he captured something - a flock of teal in for the winter from the Baltic or Siberia.

00:33:57.680 --> 00:34:06.600
I'm leaving the water park now, and heading to a place just down the road where we're guaranteed to see wildlife, but for the most unfortunate of reasons.

00:34:09.120 --> 00:34:23.520
But first, in pockets across the country, hardy breeds of ponies spend much of their lives out in the wild, often on windswept and wintry moors.

00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:26.560
Now, Adam is well known for his passion for rare breeds.

00:34:25.639 --> 00:34:30.080
But one kind of wild pony in particular helped him discover that interest.

00:34:30.600 --> 00:34:38.400
I've got three older sisters, and when we were children, my dad gave us a rare breed each to get us into rare breeds conservation.

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:41.600
And he gave me the Exmoor ponies here.

00:34:40.760 --> 00:34:48.400
So we've had them on the farm for about 40-odd years.

00:34:48.040 --> 00:34:50.080
And his first three came off Exmoor - he was given them by a guy called Ronnie Wallace.

00:34:49.120 --> 00:34:56.840
And now David Wallace owns the herd, and I'm heading down there to help them with their annual gather.

00:34:55.880 --> 00:34:59.720
Lovely, aren't you...?

00:35:00.400 --> 00:35:06.600
Exmoor National Park has a wild beauty, whatever the weather.

00:35:05.680 --> 00:35:11.320
People come here to enjoy the rugged landscape and, of course, its wild ponies.

00:35:12.040 --> 00:35:15.000
Today, there's a special event.

00:35:15.080 --> 00:35:25.680
A group of volunteers are gathering to help husband-and-wife team David and Emma Wallace round up their herd of wild Exmoors.

00:35:25.200 --> 00:35:28.760
Good morning, everybody!

00:35:27.800 --> 00:35:37.360
And welcome to our annual gathering here on Winsford Hill on a typical Exmoor day...

00:35:37.440 --> 00:35:47.400
David and Emma Wallace have gathered a large team of people to help them bring their Exmoor ponies off the moor, down to their farm.

00:35:46.680 --> 00:35:48.840
And before they set off, David's just giving them a briefing.

00:35:50.000 --> 00:35:57.760
We've got people helping us today from as far away as France and all over England...

00:35:57.240 --> 00:36:00.240
So what's the plan now, David, you're splitting everybody up?

00:35:59.360 --> 00:36:08.800
Yeah, we're organising everybody, Adam, and making sure that we get an even distribution of vehicles and ponies on both sides of this road.

00:36:07.920 --> 00:36:12.880
We're hoping to find today somewhere near about 30 to 40 ponies, something in that region.

00:36:12.640 --> 00:36:16.600
And the reason for bringing them down at this time of year?

00:36:16.240 --> 00:36:29.360
It's time to wean the foals from their mothers, it's the annual time of the year where we are separating out, we need to see whether we've got lots of little girls, the fillies, or whether we've got lots of little boys with the colts.

00:36:28.600 --> 00:36:32.880
Yeah, looking forward to seeing what we've got - it's like Christmas today.

00:36:34.360 --> 00:36:40.800
Well, I remember your father Ronnie Wallace giving my dad three Exmoors when I was just a little boy.

00:36:40.040 --> 00:36:51.000
Yes, and I remember as a little boy too delivering them to your father too, up in the Cotswolds so it's wonderful that you're here today witnessing this annual event.

00:36:51.440 --> 00:36:55.360
Well, it's very exciting - and despite the weather, I'm really looking forward to it.

00:36:54.480 --> 00:36:56.520
Yeah, glad we've been able to organise a good Exmoor day for you!

00:36:57.240 --> 00:36:59.400
Right, let's go get some ponies!

00:36:58.560 --> 00:37:01.240
Yeah, let's go and be cowboys!

00:37:03.920 --> 00:37:06.520
David's team are fully briefed.

00:37:05.760 --> 00:37:09.760
All they've got to do now is find the ponies, and round them up.

00:37:10.600 --> 00:37:13.440
Which is easier said than done.

00:37:12.760 --> 00:37:22.520
There's a convoy of cars coming up the road and it's amazing to see these horses riding across the moor in thick fog, and rain.

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:24.880
I'm not quite sure how they're finding these ponies.

00:37:31.520 --> 00:37:33.320
How you getting on, have you seen many?

00:37:32.840 --> 00:37:42.440
Yes, we saw some just over the back of the hill there which seem to have moved, come up across the road already, so we're just doing another sweep of this side of the moor, make sure we've got everyone.

00:37:41.480 --> 00:37:42.800
Great.

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:42.880
All right, good luck.

00:37:41.960 --> 00:37:43.400
Thank you!

00:37:52.440 --> 00:37:57.160
Just pulled over and spotted a group of Exmoors here quite close to the road.

00:37:56.440 --> 00:38:01.920
And the horse riders and quad bikes are coming across the moor to bring them this way.

00:38:02.160 --> 00:38:12.280
These animals are quite wild, they live out on the moor all year round, and they're perfectly designed for it, they've been living out here for hundreds if not thousands of years.

00:38:11.320 --> 00:38:18.560
They've got these really broad foreheads and the rain just runs off their eyes, and their tail fans out over their rump.

00:38:17.840 --> 00:38:26.640
And they've got amazing fur, that keeps them warm and insulated even in the harshest of conditions.

00:38:25.880 --> 00:38:27.280
And believe me, out here on Exmoor it can get VERY harsh.

00:38:34.640 --> 00:38:38.160
It's not just the riders that get a thrill.

00:38:37.400 --> 00:38:41.160
There's plenty of spectators to enjoy it as well.

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:45.000
Sue, I know you've been very involved in the Exmoor Pony Society.

00:38:44.040 --> 00:38:47.040
And I've never been up for the gather before.

00:38:46.160 --> 00:38:47.400
Ah, it's your first time?

00:38:46.600 --> 00:38:47.760
It is.

00:38:46.920 --> 00:38:48.480
Oh, it's wonderful.

00:38:47.600 --> 00:38:57.640
I've been coming to watch gatherings more years than I care to remember, and I'm still just as excited, and when you see a whole group of them break the skyline, galloping in towards you, all identical, it's fantastic.

00:38:58.840 --> 00:39:01.880
How long have they lived up on the moor?

00:39:01.400 --> 00:39:08.520
We are talking thousands of years, because we think they're a relic population of the original British hill pony.

00:39:07.640 --> 00:39:12.680
The first wild ponies came to Britain over 100,000 years ago.

00:39:11.720 --> 00:39:16.240
And we think they've been here ever since, so you're seeing something pretty special.

00:39:22.080 --> 00:39:28.960
There's about 20 cantering past now, and more coming up over the horizon.

00:39:28.080 --> 00:39:29.800
I've never seen so many Exmoors in one place at one time.

00:39:36.120 --> 00:39:42.920
'It really is a spectacular sight, 'as more and more Exmoors are driven off the moor 'and into the holding area 'before the next part of their journey.'

00:39:46.280 --> 00:39:55.880
That's the first bit of the moor gathered - they now go through into the second bit of the moor, and then into the fields, into what they call the funnel, down the road to the pens.

00:39:59.520 --> 00:40:02.600
The Exmoors look magnificent as a herd.

00:40:03.680 --> 00:40:10.360
They're an enchanting and versatile breed, and can make great riding ponies.

00:40:09.400 --> 00:40:12.440
And are never more at home than here on Exmoor.

00:40:13.560 --> 00:40:15.160
They love coming out and having a gallop across the moor.

00:40:14.280 --> 00:40:18.640
They're sure-footed, they don't mind the terrain.

00:40:17.800 --> 00:40:19.040
So, yes, brilliant.

00:40:19.520 --> 00:40:22.360
And is there any interaction between them and the wild ponies?

00:40:21.400 --> 00:40:26.240
We sometimes get the free-living ponies following us on our rides, but they don't cause us any problems.

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:27.800
Wonderful, that's lovely.

00:40:30.320 --> 00:40:34.960
The team managed to gather 30-odd ponies off the moor.

00:40:34.120 --> 00:40:39.360
And now there's just one last trot down the lanes to David and Emma's farm.

00:40:42.480 --> 00:40:46.880
After a hectic morning's work, there's a well-earned reward for everyone.

00:40:47.360 --> 00:40:49.600
So, how did it all go?

00:40:49.360 --> 00:40:57.640
It went really well, actually, considering the weather today, we've gathered all our ponies off the hill, and it's been a spectacular sight.

00:40:56.840 --> 00:41:01.480
It's very exciting to see the mares coming off with their foals.

00:41:00.880 --> 00:41:07.080
And in the next couple of days, we'll be weaning the foals from the mares.

00:41:07.040 --> 00:41:13.760
And then the mares and stallions run back up onto the moor?

00:41:12.800 --> 00:41:14.200
They do indeed, yes, yes.

00:41:13.360 --> 00:41:19.760
The foals are weaned from them, they'll go back out onto the hill and enjoy a winter without a foal annoying them.

00:41:18.800 --> 00:41:21.800
And then hopefully give birth again in the spring.

00:41:22.240 --> 00:41:23.560
Wonderful.

00:41:22.720 --> 00:41:28.280
There we are, the most ancient indigenous British breed of pony, probably the toughest of the lot, gathered safely off the moor for another year.

00:41:36.280 --> 00:41:40.400
Now for one of my favourite moments on the programme.

00:41:39.600 --> 00:41:49.000
Last winter, I went to Suffolk, the landscape of award-winning author Helen McDonald, and the bestselling book which has propelled her to fame.

00:41:49.200 --> 00:41:59.920
It's the story of how training a goshawk helped her cope with the loss of her father, and she told me where it all began.

00:41:59.200 --> 00:42:03.960
'I glanced up, and then I saw my goshawks.

00:42:03.400 --> 00:42:09.680
'There they were, 'a pair soaring above the canopy in the rapidly warming air.

00:42:08.800 --> 00:42:15.080
'There was a flat hot hand of sun on the back of my neck, 'but I smelt ice in my nose seeing those goshawks soaring.

00:42:14.360 --> 00:42:20.280
'I smelt ice, and bracken stems, and pine resin.

00:42:19.360 --> 00:42:20.760
Goshawk cocktail.

00:42:19.800 --> 00:42:22.200
'They were on the soar.'

00:42:25.960 --> 00:42:36.080
Those lines are from her book, H Is For Hawk, and this is where she first saw those goshawks - Thetford Forest.

00:42:38.280 --> 00:42:40.720
Good morning, Helen.

00:42:44.080 --> 00:42:45.200
Hello, hi.

00:42:44.440 --> 00:42:45.920
Are you all right?

00:42:45.080 --> 00:42:46.560
So, this is a good place for the goshawks, then?

00:42:45.680 --> 00:42:47.720
It's a brilliant place for the goshawks.

00:42:47.360 --> 00:42:48.520
There are a good few pairs here.

00:42:47.560 --> 00:42:56.840
They're very hard to see during most of the year, but in the spring they do tend to leave the kind of forest canopy and go up into the air, and they do these amazing display flights.

00:42:57.480 --> 00:43:04.200
If you don't see them, what other signs could someone look for?

00:43:03.320 --> 00:43:10.800
They do eat a lot of crows and pigeons, and if you find a burst of white feathers, or black feathers, or jay feathers, even, often that's a goshawk kill.

00:43:10.000 --> 00:43:13.320
'These birds are exceptional predators.

00:43:12.520 --> 00:43:19.680
'Scouring the woodland at speeds of up to 50mph, 'they can bring down prey more than twice their size.'

00:43:20.080 --> 00:43:24.520
They use kind of stealth to hunt, like an Apache helicopter.

00:43:23.680 --> 00:43:26.360
They go very low level and they kind of sweep along and grab stuff on the ground.

00:43:27.840 --> 00:43:36.120
'Once widespread in Britain, goshawks had been driven to 'extinction by the end of the 19th century.

00:43:35.200 --> 00:43:41.120
'But since the 1960s, 'a small population has been slowly re-establishing itself.

00:43:40.160 --> 00:43:44.680
'There's now around 400 breeding pairs in the UK.'

00:43:48.080 --> 00:43:50.280
It's all a bit quiet out there this morning.

00:43:49.720 --> 00:43:53.360
It is quiet, but this is the right time of day to see goshawks.

00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:59.120
You know, it's a sunny morning, and now's the time when they'll start to leave the forest and spiral up into the sky.

00:43:58.280 --> 00:44:02.000
Why did you decide to get a goshawk?

00:44:01.080 --> 00:44:03.040
They're quite feisty, aren't they?

00:44:02.800 --> 00:44:09.840
I'd been a falconer for many, many years, on and off, and I'd trained a lot of different hawks, but never wanted a goshawk.

00:44:08.960 --> 00:44:13.600
You know, they're legendarily highly strung, they're incredibly ferocious and difficult to train.

00:44:12.800 --> 00:44:20.840
My father died very suddenly in 2007, and suddenly I started dreaming of goshawks.

00:44:20.040 --> 00:44:22.680
And that was all I wanted to do.

00:44:21.760 --> 00:44:23.160
I just really knew that I needed to train a goshawk.

00:44:23.640 --> 00:44:26.920
It must have been a very tricky time during training, not least because you were grieving for your father.

00:44:26.120 --> 00:44:28.800
What can you remember from that time?

00:44:28.880 --> 00:44:36.560
When you train a hawk, it's a very complicated and difficult and ancient process, and it involves you withdrawing from the world of humans.

00:44:35.800 --> 00:44:43.680
But, eventually, I got her to fly free, and I was watching her fly and hunt like a wild hawk, and that was a much greater distraction.

00:44:43.040 --> 00:44:44.200
That was...

00:44:43.280 --> 00:44:46.560
That was like losing myself in the wild, you know?

00:44:45.960 --> 00:44:49.800
I saw the world through her eyes, and all the things that I wanted to be, she was.

00:44:49.000 --> 00:44:54.560
She didn't suffer grief, she wasn't human, she didn't have any sense of the past or future, you know.

00:44:53.600 --> 00:44:54.760
She was a kind of addiction.

00:44:54.680 --> 00:44:57.800
And I think that was a tonic to my soul, really, being with that incredible creature.

00:44:59.040 --> 00:45:06.480
Helen called her hawk Mabel, but long before Mabel, even as a child, she was fascinated by birds of prey.

00:45:06.800 --> 00:45:11.480
'I was sure they were the best things that had ever existed.

00:45:10.520 --> 00:45:17.520
'My parents thought this obsession would go the way of the others - 'dinosaurs, ponies, volcanoes.

00:45:16.680 --> 00:45:18.680
It didn't.

00:45:17.800 --> 00:45:19.280
It worsened.

00:45:18.400 --> 00:45:24.800
'When I was six, I tried to sleep every night with my arms 'folded behind my back, like wings.

00:45:24.240 --> 00:45:28.360
'This didn't last long because it is very hard to sleep 'with your arms folded behind your back like wings.'

00:45:33.960 --> 00:45:40.480
Just down the road at the Suffolk Owl Sanctuary, they're looking to instil that same enthusiasm in today's visitors from a local primary school.

00:45:43.560 --> 00:45:50.400
This is Willow, a Harris hawk...

00:45:50.280 --> 00:45:54.240
..flown by Andy Hume, the sanctuary manager.

00:45:55.280 --> 00:45:57.360
She has incredible eyesight.

00:45:57.240 --> 00:46:02.680
And it's roughly between five and six times better than our own.

00:46:01.880 --> 00:46:09.520
If you had the eyesight of Willow, you'd be able to read your favourite magazine from about half a mile away.

00:46:08.640 --> 00:46:12.560
She's got these huge pointed things here.

00:46:11.640 --> 00:46:13.480
They're not claws.

00:46:12.600 --> 00:46:14.960
What are they called?

00:46:14.160 --> 00:46:16.200
Talons.

00:46:15.440 --> 00:46:17.360
Good lad, well done.

00:46:19.000 --> 00:46:24.440
It's easy to see why Helen fell in love with these incredible creatures.

00:46:23.680 --> 00:46:27.640
Andy's agreed to let me fly Willow.

00:46:29.000 --> 00:46:34.320
So, when you call her, I'll get you to put your arm out dead straight, nice and level, so it's like a branch of a tree.

00:46:33.760 --> 00:46:34.920
Yeah.

00:46:33.960 --> 00:46:36.600
And you're going to look over your shoulder at the oncoming bird.

00:46:35.800 --> 00:46:37.000
OK.

00:46:42.200 --> 00:46:44.040
OK, so if you put your arm out straight, nice and level.

00:46:50.480 --> 00:46:52.720
What an elegant bird!

00:46:51.760 --> 00:46:54.680
That was beautiful!

00:46:55.640 --> 00:47:00.440
'It's an amazing feeling.

00:47:00.280 --> 00:47:07.080
'And meeting Willow today is bringing back 'memories for Helen of her goshawk, Mabel.'

00:47:07.320 --> 00:47:09.760
'Two enormous eyes.

00:47:08.920 --> 00:47:12.320
'My heart jumped sideways.

00:47:11.680 --> 00:47:15.200
She's a conjuring trick.

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:18.000
'A reptile, a fallen angel.

00:47:17.280 --> 00:47:24.640
'A griffin from the pages of an illuminated bestiary, 'something bright and distant, like gold falling through water.'

00:47:26.040 --> 00:47:32.480
'Goshawks can live up to 20 years, but Mabel only lived for six.

00:47:31.640 --> 00:47:36.920
'Helen hasn't flown a goshawk since then.

00:47:35.960 --> 00:47:39.640
'And today we've got a surprise for her.'

00:47:39.080 --> 00:47:41.480
He's gorgeous.

00:47:40.560 --> 00:47:43.080
He's really, really pretty.

00:47:42.200 --> 00:47:44.800
'A magnificent male goshawk.

00:47:44.040 --> 00:47:50.640
'Happy enough sat on the wrist of falconer Matt Lott, 'but these birds won't just let anybody handle them.'

00:47:49.840 --> 00:47:52.480
Here's the moment of truth, then.

00:47:59.560 --> 00:48:01.000
There we are.

00:48:00.040 --> 00:48:01.600
Oh, he's fine.

00:48:00.760 --> 00:48:04.320
We really weren't sure how this was going to go.

00:48:03.440 --> 00:48:06.160
You can't get your eyes off it.

00:48:05.240 --> 00:48:09.520
You see that intense predatory kind of curiosity.

00:48:08.560 --> 00:48:15.360
Goshawks are always looking around, always wanting to see what's there, can I hunt it, can I kill it, can I eat it?

00:48:14.640 --> 00:48:16.000
'Now the real test.

00:48:15.120 --> 00:48:18.240
'Will the goshawk fly to Helen?

00:48:25.160 --> 00:48:27.040
'The first since Mabel.'

00:48:39.360 --> 00:48:42.320
Just like old times, Helen.

00:48:41.440 --> 00:48:43.160
How fabulous.

00:48:42.320 --> 00:48:44.720
Magic.

00:48:43.840 --> 00:48:45.920
Oh, he's a sweetie.

00:48:46.920 --> 00:48:49.560
'What a beautiful moment.

00:48:49.000 --> 00:48:55.040
'But next, I'm heading to a place that gives our favourite wildlife 'a helping hand when it needs it most.'

00:49:11.920 --> 00:49:18.000
We've been looking back at some of our close encounters with winter wildlife.

00:49:17.200 --> 00:49:22.160
And I've been spotting the wintering birds that can be found at the Wiltshire end of the Cotswold Water Park.

00:49:23.120 --> 00:49:32.160
But now I've headed just a few miles south to a place where wildlife only ends up if there's a problem.

00:49:35.680 --> 00:49:41.640
Oak and Furrows Wildlife Rescue Centre is a small charity.

00:49:44.480 --> 00:49:51.760
They take in all kinds of native wildlife, and animal carer Katia Whitfield has one special patient to introduce me to.

00:49:53.480 --> 00:49:59.680
We've had two tawny owls brought in recently that we've got in here at the moment.

00:49:58.720 --> 00:50:03.960
This one was found about three or four weeks ago on the side of the road.

00:50:03.200 --> 00:50:04.520
It had a knock to the head.

00:50:03.680 --> 00:50:06.200
Can we have a look?

00:50:05.480 --> 00:50:06.800
Yes, certainly.

00:50:06.040 --> 00:50:10.440
And has a bit of concussion, and a bit of bleeding in the eye.

00:50:11.600 --> 00:50:13.080
Oh, goodness.

00:50:12.120 --> 00:50:16.840
So, what's your plan with this one?

00:50:15.880 --> 00:50:20.400
She's almost back to full strength.

00:50:19.520 --> 00:50:29.120
So her next step is to just go out into an aviary and strengthen up the wing muscles, get used to being back outside again before we release her back where she came from.

00:50:29.280 --> 00:50:35.640
When she came in, she was in a very poorly state, and she was rolling around a bit, and we weren't sure she was going to make it.

00:50:34.680 --> 00:50:37.480
But I'm very pleased to say that her chances look very, very good.

00:50:43.600 --> 00:50:44.880
You've done a good job.

00:50:44.000 --> 00:50:45.880
She looks in good condition.

00:50:44.920 --> 00:50:47.560
'This is a busy place.

00:50:46.760 --> 00:50:50.360
They care for more than 3,000 casualties a year.

00:50:49.480 --> 00:50:52.840
'Only last year, the centre moved to these new and bigger premises.

00:50:53.080 --> 00:50:57.200
'It was founded by Serena Stevens more than 20 years ago.'

00:50:58.520 --> 00:51:01.760
What was it that prompted you to start the wildlife rescue centre?

00:51:01.040 --> 00:51:06.920
It started in 1994, and, basically, what had happened is a starling had got stuck behind our brickwork.

00:51:06.400 --> 00:51:09.520
It had fallen down.

00:51:08.640 --> 00:51:09.840
We hand-reared the starling.

00:51:08.880 --> 00:51:16.400
Some people, obviously, got to hear about this, and kept bringing us the odd bird or little thing that needed help.

00:51:15.640 --> 00:51:17.560
To your house?

00:51:16.640 --> 00:51:17.840
Yep.

00:51:16.920 --> 00:51:23.160
And my daughter, Millie, who loved animals and birds, basically wanted us to open a wildlife centre.

00:51:22.280 --> 00:51:25.000
And it grew and grew.

00:51:24.240 --> 00:51:29.000
Did you ever envisage it would become this formal set-up like this?

00:51:28.200 --> 00:51:31.040
No, I never ever thought it would get this big.

00:51:31.080 --> 00:51:33.280
What's your remit?

00:51:32.520 --> 00:51:34.440
Do you take in all wildlife?

00:51:34.600 --> 00:51:37.120
It is all native wildlife.

00:51:36.600 --> 00:51:46.640
We do get thrown in for good measure the odd kitten or something, and, obviously, we've got volunteers or myself that is a bit too soft and take them on but, no, mainly it is all British wildlife.

00:51:49.360 --> 00:51:54.520
'As well as nursing animals back to health, they bring in school groups 'to learn more about their work and the wildlife.'

00:51:53.560 --> 00:51:54.720
There we go.

00:51:56.280 --> 00:51:58.160
There we are, one tight ball.

00:51:57.400 --> 00:52:00.240
And that is a nice healthy-looking hedgehog.

00:51:59.280 --> 00:52:02.000
Look, you can't see his face, see?

00:52:03.720 --> 00:52:09.320
'At this time of year, 'it's hedgehogs that make up most of the patients.'

00:52:10.560 --> 00:52:11.960
Delivery.

00:52:12.120 --> 00:52:13.360
What's this?

00:52:12.800 --> 00:52:17.440
Now, these are all the little truckers, they're all hedgehogs that have been to the vets for different reasons.

00:52:16.560 --> 00:52:23.880
So they all have to go back into their little bedrooms for the night, and we have to check them, put them back into the cages.

00:52:25.680 --> 00:52:31.200
What's the chief reason for them being brought in in the first place from the wild?

00:52:30.440 --> 00:52:35.200
Most of these poor little fellas are brought in because they're found wandering around in the daytime.

00:52:35.200 --> 00:52:36.440
I see, looking for food?

00:52:35.800 --> 00:52:39.880
Absolutely trying to get up their reserves, trying to get up their weight.

00:52:39.040 --> 00:52:44.840
They know they're obviously hungry, and, as you know, last night was absolutely freezing.

00:52:43.960 --> 00:52:47.200
They try to find some bugs, or worms, or something out there.

00:52:46.280 --> 00:52:55.040
The saddest thing is people normally don't pick up hedgehogs until they are really gone downhill.

00:52:54.160 --> 00:52:56.200
And that is really annoying.

00:52:55.880 --> 00:53:02.040
And it's not the public's fault, but nine times out of ten they come in, "Oh, it's been around in the garden "for the last few days in the daylight."

00:53:02.320 --> 00:53:03.560
It's a bit late by then.

00:53:02.760 --> 00:53:04.000
You just think, "Ugh..."

00:53:03.080 --> 00:53:05.680
But it's not their fault.

00:53:04.880 --> 00:53:12.960
It's just trying to teach the public, basically, hedgehogs are nocturnal, and absolutely, no, you never see a hedgehog out in the daytime.

00:53:15.320 --> 00:53:16.400
Right, over to the cage next to Barry and Darren.

00:53:22.960 --> 00:53:28.600
Surely that will make him put on weight.

00:53:33.600 --> 00:53:40.200
'The hedgehogs in the care of Oak And Furrows will stay here 'until the spring when they'll be released back into the wild.'

00:53:44.240 --> 00:53:53.720
Well, we're only a few days into the New Year, so there's still just about time to buy your Countryfile calendar for 2016 which features the happy hedgehog on the cover.

00:53:56.680 --> 00:54:11.640
The calendar costs £9.50, including free UK delivery.

00:54:15.080 --> 00:54:25.000
You can buy yours either via our website: Or by calling the order line on: To order by post, send your name, address and a cheque to: Please make cheques payable to BBC Countryfile calendar.

00:54:32.000 --> 00:54:45.480
A minimum of £4 from the sale of each calendar will be donated to the BBC Children In Need appeal.

00:54:44.960 --> 00:54:50.000
Well, that's it from Wiltshire, and our look back at some winter wildlife highlights.

00:54:49.240 --> 00:54:52.600
Next week, we'll be in Hertfordshire.

00:54:54.120 --> 00:54:55.920
Bye-bye.

00:55:08.400 --> 00:55:10.000
Meet the monkeys turned pirates.

00:55:21.560 --> 00:55:24.280
Join Chris Packham for the World's Sneakiest Animals.

00:55:23.560 --> 00:55:24.880
Continues...

00:55:32.440 --> 00:55:33.600
There ain't nothing to talk about, do you hear me?

00:55:32.640 --> 00:55:34.080
Your mum really needs to come in and see Doctor so we can give her a free prescription.

00:55:54.280 --> 00:55:55.400
There ain't nothing to talk about, do you hear me?

00:55:54.680 --> 00:55:56.000
I need to see her!

00:55:55.160 --> 00:55:56.640
She wants to live.

00:55:56.440 --> 00:55:58.200
A female body ought to feel joy.

00:55:57.280 --> 00:55:58.440
But it so often isn't, Nurse Franklin.

00:55:57.760 --> 00:55:58.800
Did Dad tell you?

00:55:57.960 --> 00:55:59.120
She...she didn't come out right.

00:55:58.360 --> 00:55:59.680
We cherish our young women.

00:55:59.200 --> 00:56:00.440
Without them, we would flounder.

00:55:59.480 --> 00:56:04.120
Perfect!

00:56:03.160 --> 00:56:04.440
No new look for us, Sister.

00:56:03.480 --> 00:56:05.320
A new look for a New Year.
